### What is the Rotting Oranges problem?

The problem gives a grid where each cell is 0 (empty), 1 (fresh orange), or 2 (rotten orange). Every minute, any fresh orange orthogonally adjacent to a rotten orange becomes rotten. The task is to return the minimum number of minutes until no fresh orange remains, or negative one if it is impossible.

This is a multi-source BFS problem because rot can start from multiple cells simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class RottingOranges {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        int rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
        Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        int fresh = 0;

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 2) queue.add(new int[]{r, c});
                else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;
            }
        }

        int minutes = 0;
        int[][] directions = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (!queue.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
            int size = queue.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                int[] cell = queue.poll();
                for (int[] d : directions) {
                    int nr = cell[0] + d[0];
                    int nc = cell[1] + d[1];
                    if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols
                            && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {
                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;
                        fresh--;
                        queue.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }
            minutes++;
        }
        return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] grid = {
            {2, 1, 1},
            {1, 1, 0},
            {0, 1, 1}
        };
        System.out.println("Minutes: " + new RottingOranges().orangesRotting(grid));
    }
}
```

```text
Minutes: 4
```

### Why is multi-source BFS the right approach?

Multi-source BFS is correct because all rotten cells spread rot simultaneously. If we ran a separate BFS from each rotten cell, we could not measure the combined spread time correctly. By seeding the queue with all rotten cells at time zero, the BFS naturally processes them in lockstep: level 0 contains all initially rotten cells, level 1 contains all cells that rot at minute 1, and so on. The number of BFS levels is the answer.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhyMultiSourceBFS {
    // Single-source BFS from each rotten cell:
    //   - Would need to track which cell rots first at each position
    //   - Complex and error-prone
    //
    // Multi-source BFS (all rotten cells at once):
    //   - All sources advance in lockstep
    //   - Level count directly gives the time
    //   - Simple and correct

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Multi-source BFS processes all rotten cells in lockstep.");
        System.out.println("Level 0: all initially rotten cells.");
        System.out.println("Level k: all cells that rot at minute k.");
    }
}
```

### How does the fresh counter detect the impossible case?

Instead of rescanning the entire grid after BFS to check for remaining fresh oranges, we count fresh oranges up front and decrement the counter each time we rot one. If the counter reaches zero, all fresh oranges were rotted. If it is still positive after the queue empties, some fresh orange was unreachable and we return negative one.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FreshCounterDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Grid where one fresh orange is unreachable
        int[][] grid = {
            {2, 1, 1},
            {0, 0, 0},
            {1, 0, 0}
        };
        // fresh = 3 initially
        // BFS from (0,0) rots (0,1) and (0,2) => fresh = 1
        // Queue empties, but fresh = 1 => return -1
        System.out.println("Minutes: " + new RottingOranges().orangesRotting(grid));
    }
}
```

```text
Minutes: -1
```

### How is this different from Number of Islands?

| Feature | Number of Islands | Rotting Oranges |
|---|---|---|
| Goal | count connected components | minimum time for spread |
| Sources | each unvisited land cell | all rotten cells at once |
| BFS type | single-source per component | multi-source from all rotten |
| Counting | increment per flood fill | level count gives time |
| Impossible case | none (just count) | fresh > 0 after BFS |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class IslandsVsRotting {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Islands: single-source DFS/BFS per component, count components.");
        System.out.println("Rotting: multi-source BFS, count levels for time.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a grid with no fresh oranges (return 0 immediately), a grid with no rotten oranges but some fresh ones (return negative one), a single-cell grid, and a grid where all cells are empty.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RottingEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RottingOranges solver = new RottingOranges();

        // No fresh oranges: already done
        int[][] noFresh = {{2, 2}, {2, 0}};
        System.out.println("No fresh: " + solver.orangesRotting(noFresh));

        // No rotten, but fresh exist: impossible
        int[][] noRotten = {{1, 1}, {1, 1}};
        System.out.println("No rotten: " + solver.orangesRotting(noRotten));

        // All empty: no fresh => 0
        int[][] empty = {{0, 0}, {0, 0}};
        System.out.println("All empty: " + solver.orangesRotting(empty));
    }
}
```

```text
No fresh: 0
No rotten: -1
All empty: 0
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is incrementing the minute counter even when no fresh orange was rotted in a level, which overcounts by one. Another mistake is not checking `fresh > 0` in the while loop condition, which causes the BFS to continue processing empty levels and increment minutes after all oranges are already rotten.

Wrong code that overcounts:

```java
// WRONG: increments minutes even when no orange was rotted
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int size = queue.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        // ... process cell
    }
    minutes++; // BUG: increments even for the last level with no new rot
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: only process while fresh > 0
while (!queue.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
    int size = queue.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        // ... process cell, decrement fresh
    }
    minutes++;
}
```

### When should you use multi-source BFS?

Use multi-source BFS whenever multiple sources spread simultaneously and you need the time for the spread to complete. Examples include fire spreading from multiple starting points, virus spreading through a network, and shortest distance from any source to every cell. The key insight is that all sources at time zero advance in lockstep, so the BFS level directly gives the time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MultiSourceBFSUseCases {
    // Banking use case: fraud contagion
    // - Multiple compromised accounts flagged at time zero
    // - Each minute, flag spreads to linked clean accounts
    // - Multi-source BFS gives the time to flag every reachable account

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Multi-source BFS use cases:");
        System.out.println("  1. Rotting oranges / fire spread");
        System.out.println("  2. Fraud contagion in bank networks");
        System.out.println("  3. Shortest distance from any source");
        System.out.println("  4. Virus spread in computer networks");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that multi-source BFS is equivalent to adding a virtual super-source connected to all real sources with zero-cost edges. They also know that the fresh counter is cleaner than rescanning the grid, and that the time complexity is O(rows times cols) because each cell is enqueued at most once. In production at National Bank of India, the fraud contagion model uses multi-source BFS to compute how quickly a compromise can spread through the account network, with the fresh counter flagging accounts that no compromised source can ever reach.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorRottingNotes {
    // Virtual super-source concept:
    // Imagine a node S connected to all initially rotten cells with 0-cost edges.
    // BFS from S gives the same result as multi-source BFS.
    //
    // Why fresh counter > grid rescan:
    // - O(1) check vs O(rows * cols) rescan
    // - Cleaner code, fewer bugs

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior insights:");
        System.out.println("  Multi-source BFS = virtual super-source + regular BFS");
        System.out.println("  Fresh counter avoids O(rows*cols) rescan");
    }
}
```
